            <ArticleTitle>Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia presenting with low back pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>285-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a 13-year-old boy with a 3-month history of low back pain following a mild trauma. Extensive osteoporosis and vertebral collapses were seen on conventional X-ray and computed tomography scan. Laboratory findings were non-specific. Bone marrow infiltration was observed on magnetic resonance imaging, suggesting a myeloproliferative disorder. Although not diagnostic, marrow infiltration in a child with osteoporosis should raise the suspicion of leukaemia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
